# A toast longer than its box wraps to the box (up to four rows) instead of
# being clipped at one row behind an ellipsis, so the instruction at the end
# of a long message reads; and while an overlay is open the toast paints
# beneath it — the which-key box above the statusline covers it, as Rust
# paints toast_stack before its overlays (walkthrough 1.10).
# (The overlay here is the vim profile's leader tree; the standard
# profile's which-key popup is its Ctrl+K chord chain, not an overlay.)
command editor.suspend_hint
expect screen contains "mnml does not suspend"
expect screen contains "quit with ctrl+q"
command editor.use_vim
command whichkey.leader
expect screen contains "+split"
expect screen lacks "quit with ctrl+q"
key esc
expect screen lacks "+split"
expect screen lacks "mnml does not suspend"
